{"text": "\u201cHoward County [Maryland] Faces Smoking Test\"\n\u2018Washington Times (03/11/97) P. C6\n\nIn addition to the 10-count civil lawsuit Maryland's Howard County has filed against\nColumbia, Md.'s, Clyde's restaurant, in which it claims the restaurant's patrons are\npermitted to smoke in areas not separated from the rest of the establishment, (a violation of\nanti-smoking regulations), the Rockville, Md.-based Coalition for Smoke Free Maryland\nWorkplaces has mailed letters to around 10 Howard County restaurants requesting them to\nvoluntarily ban smoking. General counsel Neil Katz has stated that if they do not, he may\nsue the restaurants for violating the Americans With Disabilities Act since several patrons\nhave severe reactions that make smoke-filled bars non-accessible. \u2018The county's\nanti-smoking regulation bans smoking in the county's 300 restaurants, except in enclosed,\nseparately-ventilated bar areas.\n\nasz9gsz0zL0z",
